course: dictionary
lesson: 9912
title: Food words from other places
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words come from food that families cook in other countries. Context helps you find the meaning. «His friends try her tamales and say they're «bussin'».» Bussin' means really tasty. The friends are eating the food, so they are praising it.
explain: «Mrs Lopez brings Mia's family tamales (steamed corn parcels) every Christmas Eve.» The words in brackets are a clue: a tamale is a steamed corn parcel. Brackets often give a meaning right beside the word.
explain: Ask: what word would fit here? Then swap your meaning into the sentence. Steamed corn parcels every Christmas Eve makes sense, so you have worked out the word. Mrs Lopez also says «Eat them hot, mija», and mija is Spanish for «my girl».
fact: A tamale is a food from Mexico and Central America.
fact: A tamale is made of corn dough called masa, filled with meat, cheese, beans or vegetables.
fact: The dough is wrapped in a corn husk or banana leaf, then steamed.
fact: You unwrap the tamale and throw away the husk before you eat it.
fact: Making tamales together as a family is called a «tamalada».
exercise: «His friends try her tamales and say they're «bussin'».» What does bussin' mean here? | Too spicy to eat | Cold and hard | *Really tasty | Made of bread
exercise: What is a tamale made of? | Potato and cheese, fried in oil | Flour and butter, baked in an oven | *Corn dough, filled and wrapped in a husk | Rice wrapped in seaweed
exercise: What must you do with the husk before eating a tamale? | *Unwrap it and throw it away | Wash it and put it back on | Keep it for the next day | Eat it with the tamale
test: Mrs Lopez brings Mia's family tamales every Christmas Eve. What are they? | *Steamed corn parcels | Cold soup in a jar | Sweet cakes with cream | Fried fish
test: Families in Mexico and Mexican-American homes often make tamales together. What is that called? | A tortilla | *A tamalada | A masa | A fiesta
exam: Mrs Lopez says «Eat them hot, mija». What does mija mean? | *My girl | My friend | A kind of tamale | Eat now
